Build a finite electric field from signed vector contributions (@physics-finite-field: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Coulomb-force-superposition-and-test-charge.lingua#r_4BCJ9YGVJSTWDV9S67V2RDR1YD") { r_0024617F87039DB1C727C66540
Use [Read rectangular maps and the transpose through their dimensions](../../mathematics/core/Read rectangular maps and the transpose through their dimensions.lingua#r_00DA69E3E3C8873C7D5F0CCB01) and the earlier spatial physics to declare positions in a common frame. For static point sources qᵢ at positions aᵢ in vacuum, the Coulomb model gives E(r)=kₑΣᵢqᵢ(r−aᵢ)/|r−aᵢ|³, where kₑ=1/(4πε₀)>0. The point r must differ from every included source position. Each contribution is a vector, directed away from a positive source and toward a negative source. The magnitude falls as inverse distance squared. The empirical law and its vacuum/static domain are premises; vector algebra combines them.

The force on a specified probe charge qₜ is F=qₜE in this model. Changing the probe sign reverses its force without reversing the source field. The field is defined independently of a probe's mass; its acceleration also depends on mass and other forces. A real probe can redistribute or move sources, so the small-test-charge interpretation must be defended rather than assumed for every apparatus. Here source positions and amounts are held fixed. The calculation does not include a point charge's singular self-field or determine atomic stability.

Separate units from an exact numerical convenience. Choose positive reference length L and source-charge scale Q*. Write ξ=r/L and qᵢ=αᵢQ*. Then E=(kₑQ*/L²) times the dimensionless sum Σαᵢ(ξ−aᵢ/L)/|ξ−aᵢ/L|³. The potential scale used next is kₑQ*/L. Setting the dimensionless prefactor to one in a program is not asserting kₑ=1 in SI units. No current numerical estimate of ε₀ is needed to check the scaled examples.

Put sources +2Q* at x=0 and −Q* at x=3L. At ξ=1 the scaled field is 2+1/4=9/4 in the positive x direction. At ξ=2 it is 1/2+1=3/2, again positive. Between the sources both contributions point the same way even though their charges have opposite signs. On other intervals, write the displacement signs afresh. A scalar sum of absolute force magnitudes would fail where the contributions oppose one another.

A planar check makes the vector issue visible. Put equal scaled sources +1 at (−3,0) and (3,0), and evaluate at (0,4). Both distances are 5. The horizontal components cancel and the scaled field is (0,8/125). With the right-hand source changed to −1, the vertical components cancel and the field is (6/125,0). Reflection symmetry predicts the zero component before arithmetic. This is a two-dimensional slice of the ordinary three-dimensional point-source law.

**Work:** derive each result from displacement vectors. Evaluate a reversed probe sign and distinguish force from field. Show how translating all positions equally preserves the predicted local field components in an unchanged axis orientation. Examine the far distance of the unequal axial pair: its net charge is Q*, so its leading distant field has a nonzero monopole contribution; a complete error-controlled multipole theory remains later work. Identify distances where a finite source size or source response would invalidate a chosen point approximation. Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) sections 5.3–5.4, then the bounded common reading of [Coulomb force, superposition and a test charge](../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Coulomb-force-superposition-and-test-charge.lingua#r_4BCJ9YGVJSTWDV9S67V2RDR1YD); fuller distributions and three-dimensional stability arguments remain in the specialist formation.

**Continue:** [Coulomb force, superposition and a test charge](../electromagnetism/electrostatics/Coulomb-force-superposition-and-test-charge.lingua#r_4BCJ9YGVJSTWDV9S67V2RDR1YD).

